window.CHTML=window.CHTML||{};window.CHTML.cb=window.CHTML.cb||{};window.CHTML.cb[20]={"h":"<h1 data-b=\"1\" id=\"b1\">Wooldridge, Chapter 5: Instrumental Variables and Two-Stage Least Squares</h1>\n<div class=\"box keybox\"><div class=\"bt\">Source line</div>\n<p data-b=\"2\"> Jeffrey M. Wooldridge, <em>Econometric Analysis of Cross Section and Panel Data</em>, 2nd ed. (MIT Press, 2010), Chapter 5, “Instrumental variables estimation of single-equation linear models”, printed pp. 83–113 (your file, 741 PDF pages, carries the printed page numbers in the running heads). I read sections 5.1–5.2 closely, including 5.2.6 on pitfalls; I did not work the end-of-chapter problems. The overidentification test, which Wooldridge puts in Section 6.2.2 (p. 122), is included here because it belongs with 2SLS; I mark where I go beyond Chapter 5.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 data-b=\"3\" id=\"b3\">What problem does IV solve?</h2>\n<p data-b=\"4\">Wooldridge’s population model is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> (5.1) with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> (5.2), but <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></math> may be correlated with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>u</mi></math> (p. 83). OLS is then inconsistent for <em>all</em> the coefficients (p. 83), by Section 19.5 of Chapter 19. An <strong>instrument</strong> <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> must satisfy two conditions (pp. 83–84):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"5\"><strong>Exogeneity:</strong> <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (5.3).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"6\"><strong>Relevance:</strong> <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> is correlated with <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></math> <em>after partialling out</em> the other exogenous regressors. In Wooldridge’s notation: write the linear projection <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></math> and require <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"7\">Condition 1 <strong>cannot be tested</strong> when the model is exactly identified (Wooldridge says so explicitly); it is an assumption that must be argued from economics. Condition 2 can be tested by an <math display=\"inline\"><mi>F</mi></math>-test in the first-stage regression.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"8\" id=\"b8\">The simple IV estimator: derivation</h2>\n<p data-b=\"9\">Take one regressor with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, instrument <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math>. Subtract expectations and use <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow></math> Replacing population covariances by sample covariances gives <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>z</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>z</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. In general, with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> instruments, the <math display=\"inline\"><mi>K</mi></math> population orthogonality conditions <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> give <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"10\" id=\"b10\">Sanity check with a binary instrument.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"11\">If <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math>, then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>: the <strong>Wald estimator</strong>, the effect of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>y</mi></math> divided by the effect of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math>. This is the intuition to teach: IV rescales the “reduced-form” effect of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>y</mi></math> by the “first-stage” effect of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math>.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"12\" id=\"b12\">Two-stage least squares (section 5.1.2, p. 90)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"13\">With more instruments (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>) we must reduce the <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math> instruments to <math display=\"inline\"><mi>K</mi></math> combinations. 2SLS does this by projecting each regressor on all of the instruments.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"14\" id=\"b14\">Stage 1:</h5>\n<p data-b=\"15\">regress each <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math> (the full list of exogenous variables, including the exogenous regressors themselves), obtaining fitted values <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>X</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>.<br/>\n<strong>Stage 2:</strong> regress <math display=\"inline\"><mi>y</mi></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>X</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"16\"><strong>Result 20.1 (2SLS closed form).</strong> The second-stage OLS coefficient equals <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow></math></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"17\"><em>Proof.</em> Second-stage OLS is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>X</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><mover><mi>X</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mover><mi>X</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>X</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>. By Theorem 19.1, <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></math> is symmetric and idempotent, so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>X</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><mover><mi>X</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>X</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. ◻</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"18\">This is exactly equation (8.29)’s single-equation special case in Chapter 21: 2SLS is a GMM estimator with weighting matrix <math display=\"inline\"><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box verdictbox\"><div class=\"bt\">Two traps</div>\n<p data-b=\"19\"> <strong>(i) Standard errors.</strong> Running the second stage by hand gives the wrong standard errors. The correct residuals are <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></mrow></math> using the <em>original</em> <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> (equation 5.25), not <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></mrow></math> (Wooldridge, p. 95). <strong>(ii) Forbidden regressions.</strong> The first stage must contain every exogenous regressor of the structural equation; leaving one out makes the second stage inconsistent.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 data-b=\"20\" id=\"b20\">Consistency and asymptotic normality: the proofs</h2>\n<h5 data-b=\"21\" id=\"b21\">Assumptions.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"22\">2SLS.1: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. 2SLS.2: (a) <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rank</mtext><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>; (b) <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rank</mtext><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> (the <em>rank condition</em>). 2SLS.3: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. (Wooldridge, pp. 92–95.)</p>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"23\"><strong>Result 20.2 (Wooldridge Theorem 5.1: consistency).</strong> Under 2SLS.1 and 2SLS.2, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>S</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>β</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"24\"><em>Proof.</em> Write <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> in the closed form of Theorem 20.1: <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msup><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msup><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow></math> By the WLLN, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>D</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The bracket converges to <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, which is invertible by 2SLS.2 (it is p.d. because <math display=\"inline\"><mi>C</mi></math> has full column rank <math display=\"inline\"><mi>K</mi></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> is p.d.). Slutsky then gives <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. ◻</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"25\"><strong>Result 20.3 (Wooldridge Theorem 5.2: asymptotic normality).</strong> Under 2SLS.1–2SLS.3, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"26\"><em>Proof.</em> From the display above, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>…</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. The summands <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> have mean zero (2SLS.1) and variance <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> (2SLS.3), so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>D</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> (CLT). Let <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. By Slutsky the limit is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>′</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. ◻</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"27\">Wooldridge omits this proof (“similar to Theorem 4.2”); the algebra above is mine. The usual estimator of the variance replaces <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> by sample moments and <math display=\"inline\"><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> by <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mover><mi>σ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></math> (5.26). If 2SLS.3 fails, use the robust (sandwich) form of Section 5.2.5, as in OLS.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"28\" id=\"b28\">Efficiency: why 2SLS is the best linear-instrument estimator</h2>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"29\"><strong>Result 20.4 (Wooldridge Theorem 5.3).</strong> Under 2SLS.1–2SLS.3, 2SLS is asymptotically efficient in the class of IV estimators that use instruments linear in <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"30\"><em>Proof.</em> Let the competing estimator use instruments <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>Γ</mi></mrow></math> (<math display=\"inline\"><mi>Γ</mi></math> an <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> constant matrix; rank condition assumed). Its asymptotic variance is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. 2SLS uses <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>Π</mi></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Π</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, which is the linear projection of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math>; because <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msup></mrow></math> is orthogonal to <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> for every <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>Γ</mi></mrow></math>. Apply the matrix inequality (projection of <math display=\"inline\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msup></math> onto <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover></math>) <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mi>′</mi></mrow></msup><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>≼</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mi>′</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math> which holds because the left side is the variance explained by projecting <math display=\"inline\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msup></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover></math> and cannot exceed the total variance. Inverting reverses the inequality: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Avar</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>…</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≽</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mi>′</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Avar</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>S</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. ◻</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"31\"><em>In words:</em> among all ways of combining the instruments linearly, the combination that is the best linear predictor of the regressors wins. The condition 2SLS.3 (homoskedasticity) is what makes the best linear combination also the best <em>use</em> of the instruments. Under heteroskedasticity, GMM with a robust weighting matrix does better (Chapter 21).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"32\" id=\"b32\">Weak instruments (section 5.2.6, pp. 101–105)</h2>\n<h5 data-b=\"33\" id=\"b33\">Three facts Wooldridge stresses.</h5>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"34\"><strong>IV is never unbiased</strong> when a regressor is endogenous. Wooldridge (p. 101, citing Kinal 1980) says that, under normality, how many moments 2SLS has is tied to the number of overidentifying restrictions, so that with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> the estimator has no mean at all. (I have not rechecked the exact count in Kinal’s paper; textbooks differ by one in how they state it, so do not cite the count itself.) We rely on large-sample theory.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"35\"><strong>A small correlation between instrument and error is magnified by weak relevance.</strong> For a single regressor and instrument, <span class=\"math display\">$$\\text{plim}\\,\\hat\\beta_{1,IV}=\\beta_1+\\frac{\\text{Cov}(z_1,u)}{\\text{Cov}(z_1,x_1)}=\\beta_1+\\frac{\\sigma_u}{\\sigma_{x_1}}\\cdot\\frac{\\text{Corr}(z_1,u)}{\\text{Corr}(z_1,x_1)}\\qquad(5.36\\text{--}5.37).\n\\;\\;\\text{\\small(20.1)}$$</span> OLS has <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">plim</mtext><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>O</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Corr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. IV is worse than OLS in terms of inconsistency whenever <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Corr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Corr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Corr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow></mrow></math> (p. 102).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"36\"><strong>Standard errors blow up.</strong> <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Avar</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>/</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>R</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, where <math display=\"inline\"><msubsup><mover><mi>R</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></math> is the <math display=\"inline\"><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> of <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub></math> on the <em>other</em> regressors (5.39–5.41, pp. 102–103). A weak instrument makes <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub></math> (the total variation of the fitted values) small and <math display=\"inline\"><msubsup><mover><mi>R</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></math> close to 1. Identification needs only that <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math> enter the first stage with a non-zero coefficient; <em>precision</em> needs it to matter.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<figure data-b=\"37\">\n<img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/fig/f20_weakiv.svg\"/>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 20.1.</strong> Equation (20.1) in action (my chart). An instrument whose correlation with the error is only 0.05 is asymptotically <em>worse</em> than OLS (whose own correlation with the error is 0.30) once its correlation with the regressor falls below <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>0.30</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h5 data-b=\"38\" id=\"b38\">Worked example (my numbers).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"39\">Population: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<table data-b=\"40\">\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Case</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: center;\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: center;\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">plim of IV</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">OLS (benchmark)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">—</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">—</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2.40</mn></mrow></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Strong, valid instrument</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Strong, slightly invalid</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.05</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>0.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2.10</mn></mrow></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Weak, slightly invalid</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.05</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>0.1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2.50</mn></mrow></math> (worse than OLS)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<h5 data-b=\"41\" id=\"b41\">Beyond Chapter 5 (flagged: general knowledge).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"42\">Practitioners test relevance with the first-stage <math display=\"inline\"><mi>F</mi></math> statistic on the excluded instruments; the widely quoted rule of thumb is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> for one endogenous regressor (Staiger and Stock 1997; Stock and Yogo 2005). I give the rule from general knowledge, not from the Wooldridge pages above, which stress the partial correlation and the role of <math display=\"inline\"><msubsup><mover><mi>R</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></math> instead. Check Stock and Yogo before citing a threshold.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"43\" id=\"b43\">Testing overidentifying restrictions (Wooldridge Section 6.2.2, p. 122)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"44\">If <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> there are <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> instruments more than needed. Each <em>excess</em> instrument gives a testable implication.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"45\">Estimate by 2SLS; save the residuals <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></mrow></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"46\">Regress <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></math> on all of <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math>; let <math display=\"inline\"><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></math> be the (uncentred) <math display=\"inline\"><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"47\">Under the null that all instruments are exogenous and under 2SLS.3, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"48\"><em>Why it works (my sketch).</em> If <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for all <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math> instruments, the 2SLS residuals <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math> must be orthogonal to <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math> up to sampling error, except in the <math display=\"inline\"><mi>K</mi></math> directions that 2SLS has used up (because the normal equations force <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>X</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). That leaves <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> free directions, hence <math display=\"inline\"><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></math>. Chapter 21 gives the general and exact version (the <math display=\"inline\"><mi>J</mi></math> statistic).</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"49\" id=\"b49\">What a rejection means and does not mean.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"50\">It says that <em>some</em> instrument is invalid or the model is misspecified; it does not say which. With a single endogenous regressor and several instruments it can be a sign that instruments have different effects (heterogeneity). <strong>A failure to reject says little if the instruments are weak or few</strong>, and as Chapter 25 shows, it says nothing at all if there are too many.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"51\" id=\"b51\">Common misreadings</h2>\n<div class=\"box plainbox\"><div class=\"bt\">Five things people get wrong</div>\n<p data-b=\"52\"></p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"53\">“IV removes the bias.” It removes the <em>inconsistency</em> if the instrument is valid; in finite samples IV is biased and can be badly so.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"54\">“A significant first stage is enough.” Relevance is necessary; exogeneity is an untestable assumption in the just-identified case.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"55\">“Use as many instruments as possible.” More instruments give more asymptotic efficiency under homoskedasticity but raise finite-sample bias toward OLS.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"56\">“The second-stage standard errors are correct.” They are not (equation 5.25).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"57\">“Lagged values are automatically valid instruments.” They are valid only if the error is serially uncorrelated enough (Chapter 23).</p></li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n<h2 data-b=\"58\" id=\"b58\">What it means for the thesis</h2>\n<p data-b=\"59\"><em>My inference.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"60\">In a bank panel, climate-related variables (transition-risk exposure, carbon intensity of lending) are plausibly correlated with unobserved bank strategy captured in the error. Any claim that an instrument is exogenous has to be defended in the text; there is no test for it when the model is just identified.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"61\">Weak-instrument arithmetic (Figure 20.1) is the first thing an examiner may check: report the first-stage <math display=\"inline\"><mi>F</mi></math> and the correlation between instrument and regressor.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"62\">With only 12 or so public-sector banks, instrument strength must be judged from the fit, not from asymptotic theory; Chapter 26 gives a small simulation of what happens in this size of panel.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box verdictbox\"><div class=\"bt\">Do you need the original?</div>\n<p data-b=\"63\"> <strong>For the derivations and the logic: no.</strong> The proofs above are complete and, for Theorem 5.2, fill in a step Wooldridge leaves out.<br/>\n<strong>Read pp. 101–105 in the original</strong> if you will teach weak instruments: the discussion of <math display=\"inline\"><msubsup><mover><mi>R</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub></math> is short and good.<br/>\n<strong>Read Section 5.3</strong> (pp. 105–107) if your thesis proposes IV solutions to omitted variables or measurement error; I have not covered it.<br/>\n<strong>Check</strong> the Stock–Yogo threshold in its source.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 class=\"unnumbered\" data-b=\"64\" id=\"b64\">Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qlist\">\n<p data-b=\"65\">State the two conditions for a valid instrument and say which can be tested.</p>\n<p data-b=\"66\">Write the 2SLS estimator in matrix form.</p>\n<p data-b=\"67\">What residuals must be used to estimate <math display=\"inline\"><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> in 2SLS?</p>\n<p data-b=\"68\">State Theorem 5.3.</p>\n<p data-b=\"69\">Show that 2SLS with one instrument and one regressor equals the simple IV estimator.</p>\n<p data-b=\"70\">Why is the first stage required to include all exogenous regressors?</p>\n<p data-b=\"71\">Why does Wooldridge say that IV has no finite mean when <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>?</p>\n<p data-b=\"72\">Explain, using (20.1), when IV is worse than OLS.</p>\n<p data-b=\"73\">Data: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. Compute the IV slope <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and the OLS slope.</p>\n<p data-b=\"74\">A regression of the capital ratio on a climate-exposure index uses the exposure of a bank’s state-owned parent as an instrument. Write down the two conditions and say what evidence would support each.</p>\n<p data-b=\"75\">Given <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Corr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Corr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Corr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow></math>: which is more inconsistent, IV or OLS?</p>\n<p data-b=\"76\">“Instruments cannot be tested, so IV results are never credible.” Discuss.</p>\n<p data-b=\"77\">“An overidentification test that does not reject proves the instruments are valid.” Respond.</p>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"unnumbered\" data-b=\"78\" id=\"b78\">Answer sketches</h3>\n<div class=\"alist\">\n<p data-b=\"79\">Exogeneity (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>), relevance (partial correlation with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math>). Relevance is testable.</p>\n<p data-b=\"80\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>X</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"81\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></mrow></math> with the original <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"82\">2SLS is efficient among IV estimators with instruments linear in <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math>, under 2SLS.1–2SLS.3.</p>\n<p data-b=\"83\">With <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></math> is square and invertible, so <math display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo></mrow></math> multiplying by <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> leaves <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, the simple IV estimator.</p>\n<p data-b=\"84\">Otherwise the second stage uses an <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math> built with fewer instruments than the model uses; the omitted exogenous variable stays in the error and is correlated with <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math>, causing inconsistency.</p>\n<p data-b=\"85\">Wooldridge, citing Kinal (1980): the existence of moments depends on the number of overidentifying restrictions; with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> there are none, so the estimator has no expected value.</p>\n<p data-b=\"86\">When <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Corr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Corr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Corr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"87\"><math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>z</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover></math> groups: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math> mean <math display=\"inline\"><mn>1.5</mn></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mi>y</mi></math> mean <math display=\"inline\"><mn>2.5</mn></math>; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math> mean <math display=\"inline\"><mn>4.5</mn></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mi>y</mi></math> mean <math display=\"inline\"><mn>8</mn></math>. Wald: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2.5</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>4.5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5.5</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.833</mn></mrow></math>. OLS: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>5.25</mn></mrow></math>; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3.25</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2.25</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1.75</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>3.75</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>6.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2.25</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1.75</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>7.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></math>; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>; OLS <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.8</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"88\">Exogeneity: the parent’s exposure affects the capital ratio only through the bank’s own exposure (hard to defend: ownership may affect capital rules directly). Relevance: first-stage <math display=\"inline\"><mi>F</mi></math>. Evidence: institutional detail, placebo tests, comparison of first-stage and reduced form.</p>\n<p data-b=\"89\">IV: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>0.3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.333</mn></mrow></math> (times <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></math>); OLS: <math display=\"inline\"><mn>0.25</mn></math>. IV is more inconsistent.</p>\n<p data-b=\"90\">Not tested but not unarguable: one can argue from institutions, examine reduced forms, run placebo checks and sensitivity analysis. “Never credible” overstates; “never proved” is right.</p>\n<p data-b=\"91\">No: failure to reject has low power, especially with weak or few instruments, and with too many instruments it is vacuous (Chapter 25).</p>\n</div>\n","s":[[148,"b3"],[148,"b8"],[149,"b12"],[149,"b20"],[150,"b28"],[150,"b32"],[152,"b43"],[152,"b51"],[152,"b58"]]};